description:
The execution-check modules (build, lint, typecheck, test) currently only know how to run Node/JS commands. For non-JS projects they either skip or run the wrong thing. For example, therminal has clippy warnings that `cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings` catches, but vibecheck doesn't find them because it doesn't know the right command.

Add a `commands` section to `.vibecheckrc` so each project can declare its CI-equivalent commands:

```json
{
  "profile": "cli",
  "commands": {
    "test": "cargo test --workspace",
    "lint": "cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings",
    "build": "cargo build --workspace",
    "typecheck": null,
    "ci": "./scripts/ci.sh"
  },
  "coverage": {
    "path": "target/llvm-cov"
  }
}
```

When `commands.<name>` is set, the corresponding execution-check module should use that command instead of the default. When set to `null`, the module should report `not_applicable`. When unset, fall back to auto-detection (current behavior).

This is the key enabler for polyglot support — rather than building language-specific module implementations for every build tool, let the user declare what commands to run and parse the output generically (exit code, stderr for warnings/errors).

Real example: therminal's CI runs `cargo fmt --all -- --check && cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings && cargo build --workspace && cargo test --workspace`. Vibecheck should be able to run these and report findings from the output, but currently has no way to know about them.
